::Q1::What problem does a pytest fixture primarily solve?
{
~It replaces the need for assertions
=It moves repeated test setup into a reusable function
~It causes the same test to run automatically
~It converts test data into production data
}

::Q2::How does a test request a fixture named sample_orders?
{
~By calling pytest.sample_orders()
=By declaring sample_orders as a parameter of the test
~By importing sample_orders from pytest
~By passing sample_orders to assert
}

::Q3::What is the default scope of a pytest fixture?
{
~class
~module
~session
=function
}

::Q4::In a fixture that uses yield, what normally happens after the yield statement?
{
~The fixture is created again
~The test is skipped
=Teardown or cleanup code is executed
~The fixture scope is changed
}

::Q5::Where should fixtures that are shared across multiple test files normally be defined?
{
~requirements.txt
~pytest.ini
=conftest.py
~test_data.py
}

::Q6::What is a key advantage of pytest parameterization over placing many inputs in a loop inside one test?
{
~It prevents invalid inputs from being tested
=Each input case is reported separately when a failure occurs
~It automatically converts inputs into fixtures
~It eliminates the need for expected values
}